Directory structure, by what a thing does.

The previous split was modules/ and model-families/, which divided code by who
wrote it rather than what it is. A transport, a tokenizer and a kernel library
shared a directory called "common" and therefore shared a fate when it was
deleted. A scheduler with zero model constants was named spark_glm52_scheduler.c
and sat in model-families/glm52/, which is a guarantee that the next model gets
its own copy.

  api/          request surface: HTTP gateway, serving engine, service,
                compatibility shim, tokenizer
  scheduler/    admission, batching, stage planning
  cache/        KV storage and the policy over it: block store, prefix sharing,
                JIT block admission
  inference/    what runs on the GPU: kernels/ and llms/
  ring/         node-to-node: hidden state transport, TP collectives, memlink,
                RDMA and TCP backends
  runtime/      what does not belong to one of the above: workspace layout,
                launch planning, tensor map encoding, module common

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE. No per-model directory outside inference/llms/.
21,714 lines of the host C carried ZERO model constants while wearing a glm52
prefix - a scheduler, a prefix cache, a serving engine, an HTTP gateway. None of
them needed to be per-model and none of the other four models had one, so the
naming was not describing duplication, it was inviting it.

ring/ rather than pp13/. The name pp13 appears 1,562 times and encodes a 13-node
topology into a subsystem that moves hidden state between pipeline ranks, which
works for any count. The rename is mechanical and not done; the directory is the
first half of it.

cache/ IS ONE SUBSYSTEM IN THREE FILES and should be one. kv_cache.c holds
bytes, prefix_cache.c decides which sequence owns which block, work_control.c
admits blocks just in time. Two of them define block allocation - measured, not
assumed: two implementations and twenty callers, which is less duplication than
it looks like from the reference counts.
